## Overview

We scale up the previous architecture in order to improve availability, performance and reliability.  
The difference now is that **each layer of the infrastructure is separated** into dedicated servers and we introduce an additional load balancer to achieve high availability at the LB level.

The system now contains:

- 2 Load Balancers (HAProxy) configured in cluster mode
- 1 dedicated Web Server (Nginx only)
- 1 dedicated Application Server (runs application logic)
- 1 dedicated Database Server (MySQL only)
- Total: 4 servers

---

## Components

| Component | Purpose |
|----------|----------|
| Load Balancer #1 (HAProxy) | Handles incoming requests and distributes traffic to web servers |
| Load Balancer #2 (HAProxy) | Cluster redundancy – takes over if LB1 fails |
| Web Server (Nginx server only) | Serves static files, handles HTTP requests, forwards dynamic requests to the app server |
| Application Server | Executes backend logic and handles business code |
| Database Server (MySQL) | Stores persistent data, isolated for security and performance |

---

## Why each element is added

1. **Second Load Balancer**
   - Added for **high availability (HA)**.
   - Provides redundancy: if LB1 goes down, LB2 continues serving traffic.
   - Eliminates the load balancer as a single point of failure.

2. **Dedicated Web Server**
   - Separates static content and HTTP handling from the application logic.
   - Better performance and easier scaling — we can add more web servers without touching app or DB servers.
   - Security improvement: isolates user–facing entry point.

3. **Dedicated Application Server**
   - Runs business logic separately from web traffic handling.
   - Allows independent scaling of application layer (scale horizontally by adding more app servers).
   - Reduces CPU/RAM load on the web tier.

4. **Dedicated Database Server**
   - Improves data security and consistency by isolating DB operations.
   - Prevents compute load from web/app from interfering with DB performance.
   - Allows future scaling using replication or sharding.

---

## Application server vs Web server

| Web Server | Application Server |
|------------|-------------------|
| Handles HTTP(S) requests and static files | Runs program logic and executes code |
| Example: Nginx, Apache | Example: uWSGI, Gunicorn, PHP-FPM, Node Runtime |
| Fast at serving static content | Processes business logic + DB calls |
| Forwards dynamic requests to the app server | Generates dynamic content & responses |

---

## Summary

This infrastructure solves previous limitations by:

- Removing load balancer SPOF through load-balancer clustering
- Separating components for better performance & maintainability
- Improving horizontal scaling capability
- Preparing the system for production-grade high availability

